{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.4,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["PR2", "H9"],
      "pins": ["PR2.1", "PR2.3", "PR2.4", "PR2.5"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "GND", "I2C_SDA", "I2C_SCL"],
      "observed": "In the \"WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES\" block the TF-Luna LiDAR footprint/socket is drawn fully wired: PR2 (\"PB_LiDAR\") sits next to its 1.25 mm connector H9 (\"PB_1.25mm\") and its pins run to +5V, GND and onto the board's main, non-isolated I2C bus (the same SDA/SCL nets that go to the ESP32-C6 and to the EZO-RTD).",
      "expected": "The specification states the LiDAR is schematic documentation only: \"its pins are 'free' and it is marked on_board: no / excluded from the board; it is not wired into any net.\" Water level in this design is sensed by the HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor and the two float switches, and the LiDAR must be left unconnected.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "its pins are \"free\" and it is marked `on_board: no` / excluded from the board; it is not wired into any net"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES — \"Water Height Sensor\": PR2 PB_LiDAR with H9 PB_1.25mm terminating on +5V, SCL, SDA, GND (sheet 1)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The LiDAR is placed on the main I2C bus that already carries the EZO-RTD and the optional BME280/BH1750 devices. If the module is fitted it adds an unplanned I2C device (address/bus-loading contention) and changes the board's water-level interface relative to the intended design.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["T4", "T2"],
      "pins": ["T4.2", "T4.3"],
      "nets": ["ATO_VALVE"],
      "observed": "The ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch is drawn as T4, an AO3400A (small SOT-23 N-MOSFET). The AON6407 8-DFN N-MOSFET that the BOM/spec assigns to this position is instead used for the input \"Reverse polarity protection\" device (T2) in the POWER TRAIN block.",
      "expected": "Spec: \"T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch; gate driven through R10 (100 Ω), pre-driven via T4 (BT3904).\" The AON6407 should be the valve's pass device, and only one AO3400A exists in the r2 BOM (T5).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "r2 drops to a smaller discrete-switch set (T1/T4 BT3904 ×2, T2 IRLR2905, T3 AON6407, T5 AO3400A ×1)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "MAIN PUMP & VALVE DRIVERS — ATO-valve driver drawn with \"T4 AO3400A\"; POWER TRAIN drawn with \"T2 AON6407\" reverse-polarity MOSFET (sheet 1)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 24 V solenoid is switched by a much smaller device than intended (SOT-23 ~5.7 A vs 8-DFN AON6407), cutting current/thermal and Vds margin on the ATO output, while the reverse-polarity position uses an oversized DFN part. The board functions but with degraded ruggedness/rating on the valve switch.",
      "confidence": 0.3
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing deviates from the intended r2 design in two places: the TF-Luna LiDAR is wired onto the main I2C bus even though the design is documented as leaving it unconnected, and the ATO-valve low-side switch is an AO3400A instead of the specified AON6407 (the AON6407 appears instead as the input reverse-polarity FET)."
}

